Part Five

Form and Mechanism of Handling Services for Victims of Violence Article 9

(1) Forms of handling services for victims of violence organized by P2TP2A include:

a. medical services in the form of treatment and recovery of physical condition conducted by medical and paramedical personnel;
b. medicolegal services constitute a form of medical service, especially Visum et Repertum and Visum et Repertum Psychiatricum, for the purpose of evidence in the judicial process, starting from the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court levels;
c. legal aid services to assist victims of violence in undergoing the judicial process;
d. spiritual services for moral strengthening according to religion and beliefs held;
e. first psychosocial assistance to victims is conducted to identify the occurrence of violence and trauma experienced by victims; f. accompaniment of victims of violence conducted by competent persons or institutions that have expertise to conduct counseling, therapy and advocacy for strengthening and recovery of victims of violence and have cooperated with P2TP2A or other names;
g. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and provision of economic access so that victims can be economically independent; and
h. services to return and or remain in educational institutions for child victims of violence.
(2) Implementation of handling services for women and children victims of violence shall be carried out: quickly, safely and comfortably, with empathy, non-discrimination, easily accessible, free of charge and guaranteed confidentiality.
(3) Mechanisms and procedures for technical implementation of handling services for women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be organized according to Standard Operating Procedures (SOP) and further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 2

The implementation of Protection of Women and Children is based on Pancasila and founded on the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, and is based on the following fundamental principles:

a. respect for human rights, women's human rights and children's rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination,
d. protection for confidentiality, security and comfort of victims . e. the best interests of the child;
f. the right to life, survival and development, and respect for the views of the child.

